{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_phase",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "At fixed N and D, excess variance above p(1-p)/(2N) is a single symmetric saturating function of heterozygosity 4*p*(1-p), rising smoothly from the boundaries to one broad mid-frequency plateau controlled by one concentration scale C.",
      "child_relation": "Keep the same variables but replace the single-scale mid-frequency amplification assumption by a steeper onset around intermediate heterozygosity and a flatter shoulder near p≈1/2, so the D effect depends on heterozygosity with an additional shape change not reducible to one saturating phase.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "p",
        "C",
        "D"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "When N and D are held fixed and p is varied, the child predicts weaker excess variance at low-to-moderate heterozygosity but a sharper rise into the central-frequency region, followed by similar saturation near p=1/2; one shared parent shape cannot match both the delayed onset and the steep central rise across the full p range."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "h",
        "expression": "4*p*(1 - p)",
        "role": "symmetric heterozygosity coordinate controlling where both alleles are represented among contributors"
      },
      {
        "name": "base_drift",
        "expression": "p*(1 - p)/(2*N)",
        "role": "ideal Wright-Fisher one-generation sampling variance baseline"
      },
      {
        "name": "parent_amp",
        "expression": "D*(1 + (C*(4*p*(1 - p))**2)/(1 + C*(4*p*(1 - p))**2))",
        "role": "current overdispersion amplification profile over heterozygosity"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when D>0 and p is sampled from boundary-adjacent through intermediate values, especially across h=4*p*(1-p) from small values up to O(1); the contrast is strongest for moderate-to-large C where the parent already bends upward but still uses only one transition scale.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, variation over p at fixed N with multiple D levels reveals non-Wright-Fisher inflation; in holdout, the same mechanism appears as a sharper frequency-localized rise of excess variance versus heterozygosity than the parent's single smooth saturation can reproduce.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the limit where the added shape refinement collapses to the original single saturation profile, i.e. when the extra curvature control is neutralized so only one heterozygosity scale remains.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The current model assumes that family-clustering amplification of drift with allele mixing is governed by one smooth saturation phase in heterozygosity, which is too rigid if representation of both alleles across contributing families turns on more abruptly before saturating.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The frequency dependence of overdispersion effects is fully described by a single rational saturation in (4*p*(1-p))^2 with one shared curvature scale C.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "A finite number of contributing family lineages or patches can produce a more threshold-like emergence of between-family allele contrast once both alleles are present across enough lineages, while still saturating at high heterozygosity.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "p",
      "C",
      "D",
      "N"
    ],
    "rationale": "Vary p while holding N, D, and C fixed. The parent maps p to V_delta_p through the Wright-Fisher factor p(1-p)/(2N) times a single symmetric amplification profile that changes curvature only once as heterozygosity increases. A realistic refinement is to relax that one-phase assumption: in finite clustered family contribution, excess drift may stay near baseline longer when one allele is rare, then increase more abruptly once both alleles occupy enough family lineages, and then flatten again near p=1/2. This is a changed target mapping, not merely an auxiliary reinterpretation, because the scored variance-versus-p curve itself changes shape. The effect is scientifically admissible only away from the absorbing boundaries and when D is nonzero; near p=0 or 1 the required allele representation across lineages is absent so both parent and child must still vanish there. A single refit of the parent parameters can shift the location and strength of its one smooth saturation but cannot simultaneously reproduce delayed low-h behavior and a sharper central upswing over the same p range if those contrasts are present."
  },
